    {"- x1 potato"}

    {"- x1 meatball"}

    {"- x10u water"}

    {"To obtain a meatball, you will need to mix:"}

    {"- x6u egg"}

    {"- x5u flour"}

    {"- x5u animal proteins"}

    {"10 seconds"}

    {"===========( Chapter 4 - Salads and Appetizers )==========="}

    {"Light fare before the main meal is made up of salads and simple appetizers, served to stir the appetite and prepare the stomach for heavier dishes."}

    {"Fruit salad:"}

    {"- x1 bowl"}

    {"- x1 apple"}

    {"- x1 grape"}

    {"- x1 orange"}

    {"- x2 watermelon slice"}

    {"5 seconds"}

    {"Citrus salad:"}

    {"- x1 bowl"}

    {"- x1 orange"}

    {"- x1 lemon"}

    {"- x1 lime"}

    {"5 seconds"}

    {"Baked potato with filling:"}

    {"- x1 potato"}

    {"- x1 cheese wedge"}

    {"15 seconds"}

    {"Carrot fries:"}

    {"- x1 carrot"}

    {"- x5u salt"}

    {"15 seconds"}

    {"Potato fries:"}

    {"- x1 potato"}

    {"- x5u salt"}

    {"15 seconds"}

    {"Onion rings:"}

    {"- x1 onion slice"}

    {"Foreign dish called \"Queso\":"}

    {"- x1 chili pepper"}

    {"- x2 cheese wedge"}

    {"Flour rolls called \"Enchilada\":"}

    {"- x2 chili pepper"}

    {"- x1 corn cob"}

    {"- x1 raw cutlet"}

    {"Layers of bread with cheese:"}

    {"- x2 bread slice"}

    {"- x1 butter slice"}

    {"- x1 cheese wedge"}

    {"=========( Chapter 5 - Ingredients )==========="}

    {"In this chapter shall be set down the list of foods already mentioned, the ways by which they may be obtained, and various counsels useful to the cook, the farmer, and the keeper of a good house."}

    {"Wheat (flour):"}

    {"Wheat is the most widespread grain among all others, and many call it the lady of fields and tables. It may be found in the middle of the island lands in the form of sheaves, or grown by one's own labors."}

    {"To obtain flour, the grains of wheat must be ground in an alchemical mortar with a pestle. Those who do not wish to practice this craft may purchase a ready sack of flour from a merchant."}

    {"Rice:"}

    {"Rice is a foreign plant, brought comparatively recently from the eastern lands, where people pay honor to enormous red lizards, and also from the countries of sands and dunes."}

    {"It is not difficult to obtain: one may buy seeds from a merchant for growing it, or purchase a ready sack of rice. To clean rice grains of husks, one should grind a rice sheaf in an alchemical mortar, then prepare it according to the desired recipe or use it for other dishes."}

    {"Oats:"}

    {"Oats have not won great love among common folk, yet they are highly valued among warriors and serving men, for food made from them keeps hunger away for a long time."}

    {"Oat seeds may be bought from a merchant, as may already ground grains."}

    {"Sugar cane (sugar):"}

    {"This sweetness is obtained by grinding the stalks of sugar cane. Sacks of sugar are also sometimes sold by merchants, though their price can be high."}

    {"Eggs:"}

    {"Every village or fortress should, if possible, keep domestic hens, for from time to time they lay eggs needed for many dishes."}

    {"If there are no birds, one can only hope for luck and search merchants for egg-fruit seeds."}

    {"Milk:"}

    {"To obtain milk, one must milk a cow into a suitable vessel, and a bucket is best used for this purpose. Milk may also be bought from a merchant, though it is sold in small portions and is not cheap."}

    {"It should be remembered: a cow must be regularly fed wheat and watered, otherwise it will cease giving milk."}

    {"Universal enzyme:"}

    {"This enzyme is sold exclusively by merchants and is kept in bottles."}

    {"Before making cheese from milk, I advise measuring the needed amount carefully, for after use the enzyme does not vanish entirely and may be used again."}

    {"Butter:"}

    {"Butter may be made by one's own hand by mixing milk with salt and properly beating the resulting mixture."}

    {"Salt:"}

    {"Salt is acquired only from merchants, and even then in small quantities, so it should be used wisely."}

    {"Other crops:"}

    {"Other plants and crops may be bought from merchants, or their seeds may be found in the island lands and grown by one's own labor."}

    {"Necessary vessels and tools for the cook's craft:"}

    {"A kitchen knife or dagger is the chief tool of any cook. With it one butchers meat, cuts vegetables, and prepares food for further cooking. A good knife may be purchased from a blacksmith."}

    {"A cauldron is a roomy vessel for mixing liquids. It is also sold by blacksmiths and craftsmen."}

    {"A clay bowl is a vessel for serving prepared dishes at table. Simple in appearance, yet necessary in every house and every kitchen."}

    {"An apron - a cook must keep himself clean, for untidiness in the kitchen is the sign of a poor master and a lazy craftsman."}

    {"A campfire is the chief and only place where food comes to know the heat of flame and becomes fit for the meal."}

    {"Mortar and pestle are tools for grinding grains, herbs, meat, and other foods. They can be made with clay."}

    {"Muffin and pie tins - their situation is much like that of mortar and pestle: merchants rarely possess such wares, and so obtaining them can be difficult."}

potrepannaya-kniga-name = battered book
potrepannaya-kniga-desc = The treatise "Physiologus, or A Description of Beasts", volume IV.
potrepannaya-kniga-content = [head=3]On Nocturnes, That Is Vampires, and Their Bloodthirsty Nature[/head]


    {"Verily, there is no creature beneath the moon more foul and contrary to the laws of nature than the nocturne, whom common folk call ghoul or vampire. These spawn are not bodiless spirits, as drunken peasants lie, but greedy predators woven of flesh and driven by an eternal thirst for living blood."}


    {"Many imagine that a nocturne may be warded away with garlic, a holy symbol, or an aspen stake. This is a dangerous error and blatant ignorance that has cost many a self-assured fool his life. The nocturne laughs at peasant superstition. It creeps soundlessly, blending with crowd or night-dark, and strikes surer than a watchman can bring a match to a cannon. Common weapons take their flesh unwillingly - to lay such a beast to rest for certain, one must cut off its head and give the body to fire."}


    {"In battle, this bloodsucker relies upon inhuman strength and bestial nimbleness. A nocturne can crumple armor with bare hands and break through a ribcage, and its fangs tear the jugular vein with ease. Another screaming heresy claims that daylight turns them to dust. Nonsense! In sunlight a nocturne feels no worse than an honest man, fears no rays, and does not hide in damp cellars from the dawn."}


    {"Therefore, if you see the figure before you bare its fangs, waste no breath on prayers, be it day or deep midnight. Draw steel, strike for the neck, and trust only the strength of your arm, for you shall receive no mercy from a nocturne."}

